it is martin luther king jr day and on  this martin luther king jr day we have a  special edition of our partnership with  ted ideas that matter  when author anna malaika tubbs visited  the mlk birthplace in atlanta there was  something that stuck with her i left  feeling frustrated by the tour guide  script  of course mlk junior was the center of  most of the tales  and then came stories about his father  the inspiring reverend martin luther  king senior  but what frustrated me was the lack of  attention being paid to his mother  alberta christine williams king  that visit helped inform tubbs book the  three mothers how the mothers of martin  luther king jr malcolm x and james  baldwin shaped a nation tubbs visited  the dr martin luther king jr memorial  tree grove in los angeles with her own  two children and offers this essay in  honor of his mother alberta  [Music]  today i celebrate the legacy of a  freedom fighter  a leader of the ever famous ebenezer  baptist church  a person who believed that faith must  always be intertwined with social  justice  that if you were to call yourself a  religious leader  you must also stand against oppression  poverty and discrimination  this person fought such inequities  through organizing and participating in  marches and boycotts  they were an active member of the end of  naacp  passionate about using their education  as a tool to combat racism and  inequality at large  her name was alberta christine williams  king  she was everything i've already  described as well as a talented musician  and a mother of three  by celebrating her on the day she  birthed her son in a room on the second  floor of their home  i also celebrate all the mothers who  deserve their long overdue recognition  isn't it amazing how much more we  understand martin when we also get to  know the woman who birthed and raised  him  isn't it beautiful to see how she  applied her passions and identity to her  mothering and how her son then took her  lessons and shared them with the world  leading the march the reverend martin  luther king isn't it powerful to see  martin not as some single disconnected  entity  but instead as a son  who was grateful to follow in his  mother's footsteps we still advocate  non-violence a passive resistance and  still  determine to use the weapon of love  proud to build on what he learned as a  little boy sitting at the table where he  not only enjoyed alberta's delicious  meals or listened to her sing gospel as  she cooked  but also where he heard his parents  discuss the importance of voting rights  and of helping meet the basic needs of  their church members i have a dream  privileges such as coming from a family  that was well educated and connected did  not always protect a young mlk junior  from the harsh realities of jim crow  he witnessed his family being  disrespected by store owners being  harassed by officers  he felt the pain of not being allowed to  see his friends once they came of an age  where it was no longer acceptable for  blacks and whites to mingle  like most of us do when we're faced with  the ugliness of the world  when we're scared or confused  martin sought his mother's comfort and  wisdom  alberta would hold him tightly  and tell him he was as good as anybody  as worthy of dignity and respect as  everyone else  she passed on her own strategies for  addressing injustice  she encouraged him to keep his faith  to find love  and to pursue his dreams  because she knew how revolutionary such  happiness and persistence were in the  face of such hatred  when we celebrate martin  we must also celebrate alberta  we must know her story  and learn how she not only shaped her  children  but also how she shaped our entire  nation  when we do this  we also recognize the essential roles  our mothers are playing not only in  their own homes but in our society  through honoring alberta we honor the  many acts of mothering  we honor the wisdom of mothers we honor  their leadership  we honor their comfort  we honor their love  may we all think of both martin and  alberta as we think about how far we  have come  and how far we have left to go  as we come together to make this world  more inclusive  as we fight the forces that try to rob  human beings of the dignity respect and  recognition we all deserve  may we think of them both  happy king day  what a smart day indeed beautiful it was  smart what a smart and different way to  very well  really beautifully done very well done  the power of love and belief in your  children  what it can reap and the difference that  a mother can make but i like what you  said tony what a different way to honor  him on this day and i kudos to animal  ikebs who by the way is married to  michael tubbs a former mayor of stockton  clearly they have two beautiful children  a nice nice couple but i've never seen  it i've never seen his story told that  way and that's to me what makes it so  very now that you mention it i knew of  his father i had heard of it i had never  heard of his mom and that was an  injustice in its own right beautiful  tribute to both
 

